Now I have enough. Plan the implementation:

Architecture:
- `src/widget/types.ts` — unified API types: `ChartWidgetProps { mode, series, xType?... }`. Actually better: unified data model `{ x: number|string|Date, y: number }[]` per series, plus mode-specific options.
- `src/widget/ChartWidget.tsx` — the single widget. Props: `mode: 'sales' | 'monitoring'`, `series: Series[]`, `title`, `height`, etc. Internally uses `React.lazy(() => import('../engines/sales/SalesEngine'))` and `React.lazy(() => import('../engines/monitoring/MonitoringEngine'))` with Suspense fallback. Engines default-export a component conforming to `EngineProps`.
- `src/engines/sales/SalesEngine.tsx` — SVG line chart: crosshair, tooltip, legend, endpoint labels, gridlines, 2px lines, area wash optional.
- `src/engines/monitoring/MonitoringEngine.tsx` — canvas-based dense time series with downsampling (min/max per pixel bucket), crosshair overlay in HTML/SVG, tooltip. Canvas for performance with thousands of points.
- `src/widget/palette.css` — CSS custom properties with light/dark.
- `src/App.tsx` — demo: Gravity UI SegmentedRadioGroup toggle + demo data generation, passes through unified widget. Also a table view toggle for accessibility.

Validate the palette with the script. I use slots 1-3 (blue, orange, aqua) for sales series, and for monitoring maybe 3 series too (cpu, memory, network). Both ≤3 series, so adjacent-pair validation suffices but let's validate first 3 slots anyway.

Gravity UI theme: ThemeProvider theme="light". I'll support dark via data-theme attr? Gravity sets `.g-root_theme_dark` class on body. To keep it simple, I'll define palette CSS vars in my own `.viz-root` with media query + `[data-theme="dark"]` scope per palette.md. Good enough for the demo.

Let me also validate the palette using the script for slots used: blue #2a78d6, orange #eb6834, aqua #1baf7a light; dark #3987e5, #d95926, #199e70.

Run: node scripts/validate_palette.js "#2a78d6,#eb6834,#1baf7a" --mode light and dark variants.

Now the unified API design:

```ts
export type WidgetMode = 'sales' | 'monitoring';

export interface DataPoint { x: number; y: number }  // x = timestamp ms
export interface Series { id: string; name: string; unit?: string; points: DataPoint[] }

export interface ChartWidgetProps {
  mode: WidgetMode;
  title: string;
  subtitle?: string;
  series: Series[];
  height?: number;
  yAxis?: { label?: string; format?: (v:number)=>string };
  xAxis?: { format?: (ts:number)=>string };
}
```

Engines accept `EngineProps = Omit<ChartWidgetProps,'mode'>`. Widget lazy-loads:

```tsx
const engines = {
  sales: lazy(() => import('../engines/sales/SalesEngine')),
  monitoring: lazy(() => import('../engines/monitoring/MonitoringEngine')),
};
```

Both engines registered in a record — single API. Vite will code-split.

For the sales engine (SVG):
- Line chart, 2px lines, area wash 10% opacity, gridlines hairline, y ticks rounded clean numbers, crosshair vertical hairline snapping to nearest x, one tooltip listing all series, legend for ≥2 series, direct end labels (selective: endpoint value per series if space). Keep it reasonable.
- X axis: time (months). Format via prop.

Monitoring engine (canvas):
- Dense: thousands of points per series. Draw on canvas with min/max bucket downsampling per pixel column. Overlay div for crosshair + tooltip (nearest bucket). Legend. Y axis ticks drawn... on canvas or SVG overlay? Simpler: draw everything on canvas, and overlay HTML tooltip. Axis text on canvas uses text tokens. HiDPI scaling via devicePixelRatio.

Accessibility: table view — I'll add a details/summary "Таблица данных" under widget in App, or inside widget. Keep in widget: a toggle button to show table? The skill says a table view exists. I'll add `<details>` with a table (sampled for monitoring to ~100 rows).

Let me also check choosing-a-form briefly? The forms are already decided by the user (line chart for sales, dense time series for monitoring). Skip.

Anti-patterns: no dual axis, no rainbow, categorical fixed order, legend present, thin marks. OK.

Let me validate palette first, then write code.

